## Why BPMN Works Well for Us

### It's a Shared Language

One of BPMN's core design goals was to bridge the gap between business analysts and technical implementers. A finance process owner can read a BPMN diagram and verify it reflects reality, while a developer or system architect can look at the same diagram and understand the technical flow. That shared vocabulary matters when we're mapping processes that span human steps, system integrations, and AI-assisted actions.

To be transparent: BPMN does have a learning curve. The full notation is extensive, and drawing *good* diagrams — with the right level of detail and structurally sound flows — takes practice. But for the scope of what we're mapping (Level 2 complex processes), you won't need the full spec, and the investment in learning the core constructs pays off quickly.

### A Diagram That's Also a File

This is where BPMN becomes genuinely interesting for our use case. A `.bpmn` file is not just a picture — it is a structured **XML document** underneath. Every element, connection, and label in the diagram is represented as readable, machine-parseable text.

Here is a small excerpt to give you a sense of what that looks like:

```xml
<bpmn:process id="Process_1" isExecutable="false">
  <bpmn:startEvent id="Event_Start" name="Report Requested"/>
  <bpmn:userTask id="Task_Prepare" name="Prepare Draft Report"/>
  <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="Flow_1" 
    sourceRef="Event_Start" targetRef="Task_Prepare"/>
</bpmn:process>
```

This XML structure means a `.bpmn` file can be opened, read, edited, and generated by *any* system that understands text — including an AI model.

### It Renders Consistently Across Tools

Because the format is standardised, the same `.bpmn` file can be opened and rendered across multiple platforms without conversion or reformatting. Some commonly used options include:

- **[bpmn.io](https://bpmn.io/)** — a free, open-source, browser-based renderer and editor backed by the makers of Camunda. You can paste XML directly into the demo at [demo.bpmn.io](https://demo.bpmn.io/) and see the diagram instantly.
- **Camunda Modeler** — a desktop application for editing BPMN files with a visual interface
- **VS Code** — with the bpmn.io VS Code extension, you can view and edit `.bpmn` files directly inside your code editor

> 🔗 **[EDITOR NOTE: Link to demo.bpmn.io here: https://demo.bpmn.io/ — you may also want to embed a short screen recording or GIF showing someone pasting XML and watching the diagram appear.]**

This portability is a meaningful advantage over proprietary diagramming tools where diagrams are locked into a specific format or subscription.

