## The AI Angle — Why This Changes Everything for Our Mapping Exercise

Here is the part that makes BPMN a particularly strategic choice for what we're doing in Finance.

Because a BPMN diagram is ultimately just structured XML text, you can describe a process to an AI model in plain language — and ask it to generate the corresponding BPMN file. The AI produces the XML, you paste it into bpmn.io or Camunda Modeler, and you have a rendered diagram immediately.

This shifts the effort of process mapping significantly. Instead of spending hours manually placing shapes and drawing arrows in a modelling tool, a process owner can describe the workflow conversationally — "First, the analyst pulls data from the system, then they validate it against the prior month's figures, then they send it to the manager for sign-off" — and have a working first draft of the diagram in minutes.

That first draft still needs review and refinement. But it changes the starting point dramatically.

### Skills That Do the Heavy Lifting

AI assistants like Claude can be given **skills** — structured sets of instructions that tell the model exactly how to behave for a specific task. For BPMN generation, this means you can create a skill that not only generates valid BPMN XML, but generates it according to a specific standard: the right task types, the right naming conventions, the right colour coding.

Our Finance AI Guild is actively building exactly this. The skill we're developing ensures that any BPMN generated from a process description automatically conforms to our internal classification standard — distinguishing human tasks from automated ones, from AI-assisted ones, from approval steps — with consistent colour coding so that any reader can understand the diagram at a glance without extra explanation.

